  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 146 PM MDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nUpper air analysis shows ridging over the Ohio Valley, with the main \naxis just crossing into the eastern Dakotas. A strong upper low just \noffshore the Pacific NW coast is bringing active southwesterly flow \nto much of the western CONUS, including the CWA. A shortwave is \ncrossing WY at this time, with isolated showers and thunderstorms \ndeveloping over the last two hours. Surface obs (and even the KUDX \nradar) show nicely a warm front pushing into the Black Hills and \nsoutherns SD. Temperatures have risen into the 90s behind the front, \nalong with dewpoints dropping from the 60s over the western SD \nplains into the 40s. This front will continue northeastward through \nmost of the CWA by late evening. Storms may make it into the \nCWA...especially the SW portions. Because of the drier air moving \nin, instability is rather weak. However, inverted boundary layer \nprofiles behind the front would support gusty winds. If storms make \nit across the CWA border, can't rule out some gusts making it above \n50 mph (as has already occurred in the CYS CWA). While \ninstability is much higher ahead of the warm front, so is the \ncap...so storms are not expected to be an issue toward NW SD. The \nwarmer, drier air will continue to advect in through Thursday \nmorning. Highs will reach into the upper 80s and 90s with mostly \nsunny skies. A second reinforcing shot of hot, dry air will be \ndrawn into the CWA Friday morning ahead of a strengthening surface\nlow over MT. Temps well into the 90s...and even nearing 100...are\npossible Fri and Sat. After Saturday, models diverge with what \nnext week will look like. The spread of solutions generally range \nfrom near average to well above. Climatologically, early to mid \nSeptember tends to bring a noticeable shift toward cooler, more \nautumn-like (aka mild) weather. Both the 12z GFS and ECMWF are \nfinally hinting at this with a cold front pushing through during \nthe first half of next week. Will be interesting to see where \nmodels settle over the next few days.",
